  "whyItWorks": "When the chords change every two beats there is no time to think in scales. Put a chord tone on every beat and the line spells the harmony by itself; let the 3rds and 7ths step by half steps into the next chord and each change sounds smooth. The chromatic notes on the off-beats are just bridges between those strong notes.",
  "fingeringNote": "One position with one small stretch, no shifts. As written, the index finger sits at fret 7 and the second finger starts the line on the D string. In bar 1 the little finger stretches up to fret 11 on the B string, four frets above the index, and the chromatic run back down uses one finger per fret (4, 3, 2, 1), so the hand briefly sits one fret higher. Let it settle back as the line returns to the G string.",
  "practiceTask": "Play it over bars 27 to 29 of the weekly standard, the two descending ii–Vs near the end of the form. First play only a 3rd or 7th on each chord change, moving each one to the nearest 3rd or 7th of the next chord, then add the full line slowly.",
  "variation": {
    "description": "Aim one step lower: instead of enclosing the 3rd, the line falls from the 7th of {{chord 4}} through its b13 ({{note 2.8}}) and lands on the 9th of {{chord 5}}, a brighter, more open ending.",
